tickets/in-progress/agent-team-hierarchical-handoffs/requirements.md:5:`Refined — SR-013 user-approved; ready for architecture re-review of CR-F-011` — SR-012 passed architecture review as ARCH-REV-007 and entered implementation. CRR-022 then proved that the persisted-transition design conflated historical display `memberName` with structural route/path identity and omitted the supported state where the stable `20260517_team_run_metadata_member_tree` record is already terminal. SR-013 corrects only that migration contract: one migration-only flat decoder preserves display names while producing/validating structural predecessor `memberTree`; the later, ticket-owned `20260801_team_canonical_identity` migration derives each canonical address exclusively from an agreeing route/path pair and deliberately does not compare the display name. Because the later migration has its own pending ID, it executes after a fresh prerequisite run and after an already-recorded prerequisite success; when a terminal-warning record left a repaired flat item behind, the canonical migration composes that same flat decoder in memory and performs one atomic flat-to-v3 replacement. Genuine structural/topology/coordinator/run contradictions still fail without source mutation, and normal runtime remains schema-v3-only. SR-012's rooted schema, V5 application boundary, collaboration protocol, live-validation matrix, and physical-storage preservation remain unchanged. The user explicitly approved the completed SR-013 package for architecture re-review; implementation remains blocked until architecture passes. SR-014 adds only the user-approved exact natural-language system-instruction copy for implementation and does not change architecture or behavior.
tickets/in-progress/agent-team-hierarchical-handoffs/requirements.md:205:- **R-041 — Structured data migration:** Framework-owned TeamRun metadata, Team communication projections, task delegation records, token-usage storage, external-channel bindings, and application platform databases shall be converted to the current canonical model with per-item/file/database validation, backups immediately before mutation where applicable, same-directory atomic file replacement or database transaction, actionable failure details, byte-stable rejection, and idempotent rerun. For historical flat and predecessor `memberTree` TeamRun data, `memberName` is display/presentation input and is never required to equal a structural segment. One migration-only flat decoder shall preserve that value while producing the structural predecessor tree; the stable prerequisite and, only for residual flat input after a terminal prerequisite record, the canonical migration shall reuse that same decoder rather than duplicate flat interpretation. The canonical converter shall derive address only after normalized `memberRouteKey` and `memberPath` agree exactly, shall retain genuine Agent/role/description fields, shall drop obsolete legacy display/name/path/route fields from the target, and shall fail missing/contradictory structural route/path, unsafe flat nesting, parent/child topology, duplicate address, coordinator, or generic/kind-specific run identity rather than guess. The application-database migration inventory shall enumerate existing platform databases from the application-data directory/store, not from the admitted application-bundle catalog, so an old or invalid bundle cannot hide required durable state.
tickets/in-progress/agent-team-hierarchical-handoffs/requirements.md:206:- **R-042 — Blocking current-schema rollout:** App-data migration ordering shall keep stable `20260517_team_run_metadata_member_tree` as the pending flat-to-predecessor record owner and later `20260801_team_canonical_identity` as the sole schema-v3 replacement owner. `runPending()` may skip the stable prerequisite when its record is `SUCCEEDED` or `SUCCEEDED_WITH_WARNINGS`, but the separately identified canonical migration must still execute when pending; implementation shall not depend on changed code under a completed migration ID rerunning. For a predecessor tree it converts directly; for a residual/repaired safe flat item left behind under a terminal-warning record it composes the shared migration-only flat decoder and canonical converter in memory, validates v3, and performs one backup/atomic flat-to-v3 replacement. After the canonical migration and before index rebuild/service bootstrap, startup shall block unless the canonical migration is exactly `SUCCEEDED`. A fresh flat installation therefore runs prerequisite then canonical in order; an installation with a terminal prerequisite runs canonical over every predecessor or residual flat item. Bundle admission occurs through exact target contract gates independently of durable-data discovery. Normal runtime readers/writers and frontend/server contracts shall support only the current schema; no dual read/write, historical runtime fallback, V4-to-V5 bundle adapter, mixed-version execution, or lazy conversion is allowed.
tickets/in-progress/agent-team-hierarchical-handoffs/requirements.md:247:- **AC-031:** The maintained real historical flat fixture with `Program Manager` / `program_manager` and `QA Specialist` / `qa_specialist` passes the ordered chain and becomes schema v3 with addresses `/program_manager` and `/qa_specialist`. A representative predecessor `memberTree` with the same display/structural divergence also converts whether the `20260517_team_run_metadata_member_tree` record is absent or already `SUCCEEDED`/`SUCCEEDED_WITH_WARNINGS`; a preseeded terminal-warning record plus the repaired safe flat fixture converts directly through `20260801_team_canonical_identity` without rerunning `20260517`. All outputs contain one `rootTeam`, typed node-local run IDs, coordinator addresses, `children`, unchanged genuine Agent/role/description fields, handoffs, timestamps, and platform run IDs; obsolete `memberName`/path/route fields are absent. Missing or disagreeing route/path, unsafe flat nesting, non-direct parent/child structure, duplicate addresses, invalid coordinator, or AgentTeam run-ID contradiction fails before replacement with actionable item detail and byte-stable source.
tickets/in-progress/agent-team-hierarchical-handoffs/requirements.md:253:- **AC-037:** Durable migration-chain tests prove: (a) fresh real historical flat input runs the stable prerequisite and then the canonical migration to success; (b) a preseeded terminal `20260517_team_run_metadata_member_tree` record is skipped while the pending `20260801_team_canonical_identity` migration converts both predecessor-tree input and a residual/repaired safe flat item through the one shared flat decoder; (c) safe files receive the required pre-mutation backups and same-directory atomic replacements, with the residual-flat branch writing only final v3; (d) current v3 and partial-rerun subjects are idempotently skipped; and (e) unsafe flat/nested/route-path/topology/coordinator/run input remains byte-stable with an actionable failure. Canonical failure or runner rejection prevents bootstrap/listen; after repair the non-terminal canonical migration reruns without needing the terminal prerequisite or a listening API. Canonical exact success, including with an unrelated warning status, starts once with strict current readers and derived indexes rebuilt afterward. Application platform-database discovery is independently proven outside V5 bundle admission, including an old V4 bundle and a persisted-only database subject.
